WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.013
Welcome to Module 1 of the Data Platform
and SQL Server Identity learning path:

2
00:00:05.013 --> 00:00:07.325
the estate and the consolidation gate.

3
00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:08.873
It teaches Runbook R oh two,

4
00:00:08.873 --> 00:00:13.375
with Chapter 1 of the developer
workbook, Building SQL Server Identity.

5
00:00:13.375 --> 00:00:16.991
Before any login moves, you
count every SQL Server instance,

6
00:00:16.991 --> 00:00:19.912
and decide which ones the
identity work is spent on.

7
00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:22.450
This is the first of ten modules.

8
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.587
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:27.587 --> 00:00:30.453
After twenty years across
an Active Directory forest,

10
00:00:30.453 --> 00:00:33.564
the SQL Server estate is larger
than anyone's list of it,

11
00:00:33.564 --> 00:00:36.674
and part of it shouldn't survive
the transformation at all.

12
00:00:36.674 --> 00:00:41.350
Migrating the logins of an instance you
retire three months later is wasted work.

13
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.643
So the identity work starts with
a count: one row per instance,

14
00:00:45.643 --> 00:00:51.137
classified retain, consolidate, retire
or review, before any login is touched.

15
00:00:51.137 --> 00:00:55.388
Only retained instances and consolidation
targets go on to the migration.

16
00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:58.156
In this module you see
how that gate is decided,

17
00:00:58.156 --> 00:01:01.590
and run the kit's classifier
over a fictional estate.

18
00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.106
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

19
00:01:08.106 --> 00:01:10.390
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

20
00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.534
You'll name the three forms of
sprawl in a twenty-year estate,

21
00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:17.053
and say why the inventory comes
before the authentication audit.

22
00:01:17.053 --> 00:01:20.557
You'll apply the three
rationalization lenses, utilization,

23
00:01:20.557 --> 00:01:24.552
version and dependencies, and say
which classes enter the migration.

24
00:01:24.552 --> 00:01:28.156
You'll explain how a recorded
constraint changes a first-pass answer,

25
00:01:28.156 --> 00:01:31.002
and why an instance nothing
could read lands in review.

26
00:01:31.002 --> 00:01:33.887
And you'll classify a
fictional estate with the kit,

27
00:01:33.887 --> 00:01:37.252
and show that a changed file no
longer matches its manifest.

28
00:01:37.252 --> 00:01:42.121
Before you start, follow the path's
get-the-code steps, and read the first chapter

29
00:01:42.121 --> 00:01:47.590
of Data Platform: SQL Server Identity,
What Windows Authentication Actually Is.

30
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.515
Unit 2 is finding the estate.

31
00:01:53.515 --> 00:01:55.465
An estate grows by accretion.

32
00:01:55.465 --> 00:01:57.965
A development instance becomes production.

33
00:01:57.965 --> 00:02:00.728
A vendor test instance
is never decommissioned.

34
00:02:00.728 --> 00:02:04.678
An installer drops SQL Server
Express on an application server.

35
00:02:04.678 --> 00:02:08.165
The data platform book's chapter,
The Shape of Twenty Years,

36
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:11.435
names three overlapping
problems: too many instances,

37
00:02:11.435 --> 00:02:15.214
too many databases, and too many
forest-spanning dependencies.

38
00:02:15.214 --> 00:02:18.128
The transformation doesn't
begin until they're assessed.

39
00:02:18.128 --> 00:02:20.724
No single instrument finds every instance,

40
00:02:20.724 --> 00:02:24.043
so discovery combines the
network and directory sweep,

41
00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:28.516
the trail of service principal names,
or S P Ns, that SQL Server registers,

42
00:02:28.516 --> 00:02:30.681
and Microsoft's assessment tooling,

43
00:02:30.681 --> 00:02:34.577
and fuses them into one inventory
that counts each instance once.

44
00:02:34.577 --> 00:02:36.753
The kit doesn't rebuild discovery.

45
00:02:36.753 --> 00:02:39.809
The Start Kit's SQL Server
count finds the instances,

46
00:02:39.809 --> 00:02:42.110
and the kit classifies what it found.

47
00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.872
What does the inventory have to hold?

48
00:02:47.872 --> 00:02:51.052
The data platform book's
chapter, The Estate Inventory,

49
00:02:51.052 --> 00:02:54.711
lists a consistent set of facts
for every instance it can find.

50
00:02:54.711 --> 00:02:59.898
The version, edition and build number
establish the support and licensing posture.

51
00:02:59.898 --> 00:03:04.286
The host operating system version
establishes the Arc and upgrade envelope.

52
00:03:04.286 --> 00:03:06.652
The domain-join state
and the owning domain

53
00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:09.085
establish the forest-entanglement picture.

54
00:03:09.085 --> 00:03:13.535
The service account and its source domain
establish the credential dependencies.

55
00:03:13.535 --> 00:03:16.885
And for every database, the
size, the last-access timestamp,

56
00:03:16.885 --> 00:03:20.444
the recovery model and the
owning application establish the

57
00:03:20.444 --> 00:03:24.422
utilization and zombie picture that
the lenses turn into a decision.

58
00:03:24.422 --> 00:03:27.322
The inventory isn't a
point-in-time spreadsheet.

59
00:03:27.322 --> 00:03:31.474
It's designed to be re-run, because
instances appear and disappear across a

60
00:03:31.474 --> 00:03:36.200
multi-month assessment, and the consolidation
plan must track a moving estate.

61
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.662
Runbook R oh two's first step sets out
the rights each depth of discovery needs.

62
00:03:44.662 --> 00:03:49.513
Read access to Active Directory finds
candidates; it doesn't complete the inventory.

63
00:03:49.513 --> 00:03:54.595
The directory depth gives candidate hosts
and instances from S P Ns and directory

64
00:03:54.595 --> 00:03:56.539
objects, and needs nothing more:

65
00:03:56.539 --> 00:04:00.126
the SQL Server Browser probe
needs only a network path.

66
00:04:00.126 --> 00:04:04.216
The host depth gives the instances
installed and each service's account,

67
00:04:04.216 --> 00:04:08.512
and needs local administrator by default,
or a least-privilege alternative.

68
00:04:08.512 --> 00:04:12.868
The instance depth gives the version,
the mode, every login and the databases,

69
00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:16.463
and needs a Windows login with
the Start Kit's read permissions.

70
00:04:16.463 --> 00:04:17.712
Never sysadmin.

71
00:04:17.712 --> 00:04:20.225
Azure Arc is an optional fourth depth.

72
00:04:20.225 --> 00:04:24.128
Run every depth under one dedicated
discovery identity per forest,

73
00:04:24.128 --> 00:04:28.797
a group managed service account for
scheduled runs, and add the host and instance

74
00:04:28.797 --> 00:04:33.500
rights for the assessment window only,
recording each grant and its removal.

75
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.100
Unit 3 is the gate.

76
00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.887
Three lenses turn the
inventory into a judgment.

77
00:04:41.887 --> 00:04:46.591
Utilization finds zombies: a database
with no connections in four hundred

78
00:04:46.591 --> 00:04:50.838
or more days, no restores consumed,
and a retired owning application.

79
00:04:50.838 --> 00:04:53.982
Version decides what can
take Entra logins at all,

80
00:04:53.982 --> 00:04:58.737
since the FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER
syntax needs SQL Server 2022 or later.

81
00:04:58.737 --> 00:05:01.591
And dependencies, such as linked servers,

82
00:05:01.591 --> 00:05:06.626
replication and S S I S packages, are
what most often overturn a naive plan.

83
00:05:06.626 --> 00:05:12.525
The result is one class per instance:
retain, consolidate into a target, or retire.

84
00:05:12.525 --> 00:05:14.300
One caution from R oh two.

85
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.438
The index-usage counters
reset when the engine starts,

86
00:05:17.438 --> 00:05:21.412
so a database on an instance that
restarted last week can look unused.

87
00:05:21.412 --> 00:05:25.798
Read the start time, and take the
long-horizon evidence from login auditing,

88
00:05:25.798 --> 00:05:30.850
backup history and the application portfolio,
before you call anything a zombie.

89
00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.732
The kit's classifier follows
R oh two's first pass,

90
00:05:37.732 --> 00:05:40.275
then applies the recorded constraints.

91
00:05:40.275 --> 00:05:44.126
An instance whose every database
is idle past the zombie threshold,

92
00:05:44.126 --> 00:05:47.887
or whose every owning application
is decommissioned, is retired.

93
00:05:47.887 --> 00:05:51.551
An instance with no version goes
to review, never to retain.

94
00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:56.810
A version before SQL Server 2022
consolidates into a shared target,

95
00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.950
and 2022 or later is retained.

96
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.313
Then the constraints.

97
00:06:00.313 --> 00:06:05.168
Collation conflicts, version-locked vendor
applications, compliance isolation,

98
00:06:05.168 --> 00:06:09.167
indivisible dependencies and
failover cluster instances turn an

99
00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:13.237
apparent consolidation into a
dedicated target, or a retain in place.

100
00:06:13.237 --> 00:06:19.320
It's a first pass: the architects confirm
or override every row, and record why.

101
00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.007
Try it.

102
00:06:24.007 --> 00:06:27.478
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7 on
the kit's fictional estate,

103
00:06:27.478 --> 00:06:29.433
with no instance and no tenant.

104
00:06:29.433 --> 00:06:33.450
Go to the kit's folder, and make this
run's results folder, named for the

105
00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:37.469
time you start in U T C, so running
the lab again never overwrites a run.

106
00:06:37.469 --> 00:06:41.220
Step one classifies eight
instances for contoso dot example.

107
00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.570
Look at the reasons.

108
00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:48.058
Sequel P R D oh one and oh two run
SQL Server 2022 and are retained,

109
00:06:48.058 --> 00:06:53.745
and sequel P R D oh six, on 2025, is
retained as the consolidation target.

110
00:06:53.745 --> 00:06:57.805
Sequel L E G oh three, on
2016, can't take Entra logins,

111
00:06:57.805 --> 00:07:01.270
so it consolidates into the shared target.

112
00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:06.832
Sequel O L D oh five is retired: every
database idle four hundred days or more.

113
00:07:06.832 --> 00:07:11.819
Sequel L E G oh seven's collation
conflict sends it to a dedicated target.

114
00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:15.545
Sequel D E V oh eight has no
version, so it's in review.

115
00:07:15.545 --> 00:07:18.567
And the denominator
line counts each class:

116
00:07:18.567 --> 00:07:22.345
two consolidate, four retain,
one retire and one review.

117
00:07:22.345 --> 00:07:26.645
Sequel F C I oh four is retained only
because of its vendor constraint.

118
00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:31.840
It's also a failover cluster instance,
which Chapter 3 puts in Category 3.

119
00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.727
Step two opens the manifest.

120
00:07:37.727 --> 00:07:41.390
It names the step, estate, and
says the run was read-only.

121
00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:46.032
It names the step's control, C M
eight, and its K S I, and the S H A

122
00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:51.365
two fifty-six hash of each file the
run wrote: the C S V and the jay-son.

123
00:07:51.365 --> 00:07:55.677
Step three imports the kit's module and
checks the folder against its manifest.

124
00:07:55.677 --> 00:07:59.039
Both files match the hashes
recorded when the run wrote them.

125
00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:03.795
This is why the manifest matters to an
assessor: the I S S O keeps the run folder,

126
00:08:03.795 --> 00:08:06.210
and reruns the check on the stored copy,

127
00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:09.320
so the inventory kept is
the one the run wrote.

128
00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.670
Step four looks at the two instances
that don't enter the migration.

129
00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:21.688
Sequel O L D oh five's only database was
last used nine hundred and twelve days ago,

130
00:08:21.688 --> 00:08:25.220
well past the four-hundred-day
threshold, so it's retired.

131
00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:28.353
Sequel D E V oh eight is
known only by its S P N:

132
00:08:28.353 --> 00:08:31.933
discovery found it, but
nothing read its version.

133
00:08:31.933 --> 00:08:36.433
It stays in review until someone reads
it, or confirms the host is gone.

134
00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:38.450
Review is never retain.

135
00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.618
Step five copies the run's folder,

136
00:08:44.618 --> 00:08:48.388
adds a row by hand to the copy's
C S V, and checks it again.

137
00:08:48.388 --> 00:08:53.226
The C S V no longer matches its
recorded hash; the jay-son still does.

138
00:08:53.226 --> 00:08:56.712
That's how the manifest catches
a file changed after the run.

139
00:08:56.712 --> 00:09:00.674
Step six runs this chapter's
tests: eight pass, none fail.

140
00:09:00.674 --> 00:09:04.613
Step seven lists your runs,
one line per run, oldest first.

141
00:09:04.613 --> 00:09:08.143
There's nothing to clean up: the
kit's folder is as you unzipped it,

142
00:09:08.143 --> 00:09:10.890
and this run's results
stay in its own folder.

143
00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:17.090
Lab 2 runs on your own test domain.

144
00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.065
It hasn't been run by the authors.

145
00:09:19.065 --> 00:09:24.132
You need a test domain with at least one
test SQL Server instance, never production;

146
00:09:24.132 --> 00:09:27.415
a repository checkout, so the
Start Kit is beside the kit;

147
00:09:27.415 --> 00:09:31.768
PowerShell 7 with the Active Directory
module on a domain-joined machine;

148
00:09:31.768 --> 00:09:35.622
a network path to the instance's
port and U D P fourteen thirty-four;

149
00:09:35.622 --> 00:09:39.190
and a Windows login holding the
Start Kit's read permissions.

150
00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.440
Never sysadmin.

151
00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.206
Run the Start Kit's discovery,

152
00:09:42.206 --> 00:09:45.590
turn its output into the kit's
inventory, and classify it.

153
00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:48.542
Compare each row with the
Start Kit's own first pass,

154
00:09:48.542 --> 00:09:52.690
add the owners' constraints and
application status, and classify again.

155
00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.715
The second run is the one to keep.

156
00:09:54.715 --> 00:09:57.653
Nothing on the instance or
in the directory changes.

157
00:09:57.653 --> 00:10:01.048
To back it out, delete the lab's
files, and have the administrator

158
00:10:01.048 --> 00:10:04.620
remove the read permissions if
they were granted for the lab.

159
00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.020
The federal requirement.

160
00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.650
Chapter 1's table maps the classification
and the manifest check to C M eight,

161
00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:21.708
System Component Inventory, in NIST SP
800-53, with the K S I for the inventory.

162
00:10:21.708 --> 00:10:26.822
Classifying every instance and counting
the denominator once supports C M eight,

163
00:10:26.822 --> 00:10:30.057
with the C S V, the jay-son
and the manifest as evidence.

164
00:10:30.057 --> 00:10:33.505
Showing that a changed file no
longer matches its run provides

165
00:10:33.505 --> 00:10:36.620
evidence that the inventory
kept is the one the run wrote.

166
00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:40.672
Runbook R oh two also maps the rights
each depth of discovery needs,

167
00:10:40.672 --> 00:10:43.030
and its reconciliation with Azure Arc.

168
00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:47.082
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers:

169
00:10:47.082 --> 00:10:49.882
the agency lines its own
evidence up with them.

170
00:10:49.882 --> 00:10:52.706
The run's files provide
evidence for a control;

171
00:10:52.706 --> 00:10:56.880
the agency still sets its
parameters, and the assessor decides.

172
00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:02.730
Check yourself with two questions.

173
00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:06.504
First: sequel D E V oh eight
was found only by its S P N,

174
00:11:06.504 --> 00:11:08.742
and nothing read its version.

175
00:11:08.742 --> 00:11:10.405
How does the kit classify it?

176
00:11:10.405 --> 00:11:15.755
The answer is c: review, until someone
reads it or confirms the host is gone.

177
00:11:15.755 --> 00:11:18.467
No version means review, never retain.

178
00:11:18.467 --> 00:11:24.842
Second: sequel F C I oh four runs SQL
Server 2019, yet the kit retains it.

179
00:11:24.842 --> 00:11:25.555
Why?

180
00:11:25.555 --> 00:11:29.483
The answer is a: a recorded vendor
constraint keeps it in place,

181
00:11:29.483 --> 00:11:32.255
and an upgrade or
exception is still needed.

182
00:11:32.255 --> 00:11:34.393
That's rule six of the classifier.

183
00:11:34.393 --> 00:11:39.040
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

184
00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:43.865
To sum up.

185
00:11:43.865 --> 00:11:48.622
You learned why the estate is counted
and rationalized before any login moves,

186
00:11:48.622 --> 00:11:53.503
and how the gate sorts every instance into
retain, consolidate, retire or review.

187
00:11:53.503 --> 00:11:55.618
You classified a fictional estate,

188
00:11:55.618 --> 00:11:58.902
and saw its manifest catch a
file changed after the run.

189
00:11:58.902 --> 00:12:03.387
The evidence an I S S O keeps from this
module is the run folder, the C S V,

190
00:12:03.387 --> 00:12:06.591
the jay-son and the manifest
with each file's hash,

191
00:12:06.591 --> 00:12:10.915
and the manifest check's output
beside it, rerun on the stored copy.

192
00:12:10.915 --> 00:12:13.828
Before shipping, work through
Chapter 1's checklist.

193
00:12:13.828 --> 00:12:16.840
Next is Module 2, the
authentication audit.
